.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="None of physicskit.classical&#x27;s integrators are adaptive: each takes a fixed step \Delta t for the whole run, so choosing \Delta t is normally a manual &quot;integrate, check drift, halve or double, repeat&quot; search -- exactly what estimate_dt automates, by searching for the largest \Delta t whose relative energy drift |H(t) - H(0)| / |H(0)| stays below a given tolerance over the full run. This runs it on two very different conservative systems: a quasi-periodic Kepler orbit (~physicskit.classical.systems.newtonian.KeplerSystem, semi-major axis a=1, eccentricity e=0.5, governed by the inverse-square gravitational Hamiltonian), where a short probe already predicts the right \Delta t, and a chaotic double pendulum (~physicskit.classical.systems.lagrangian.DoublePendulum, governed by its coupled Euler-Lagrange equations), where estimate_dt&#x27;s own verification stage has to catch and shrink a fast-probe estimate that would otherwise fail once actually run for the full step count.">

